Experiment 6: Explore Docker Commands for Container Management
Aim
To explore basic Docker commands used to manage containers.

Step 1: Check Docker installation
Open Command Prompt and type:

docker --version
Expected output looks like:

Docker version ...
If Docker isn't installed, you'll need to install and start Docker Desktop first.

Step 2: Try these important Docker commands
1. Check Docker information

docker info
2. Download a sample image

docker pull hello-world
3. Run a container

docker run hello-world
Expected output includes:

Hello from Docker!
4. List running containers

docker ps
5. List all containers

docker ps -a
6. List downloaded images

docker images
7. Remove a container

First, list the containers:

docker ps -a
Copy the container ID, then run:

docker rm CONTAINER_ID
Replace CONTAINER_ID with the actual ID.

Easy way to remember
Command	Purpose
docker pull	Download an image
docker run	Create and run a container
docker ps	List running containers
docker ps -a	List all containers
docker images	List images
docker stop	Stop a running container
docker rm	Remove a container
Result: Basic Docker commands were explored to download images, run containers, and manage containers.

Experiment 7: Develop a Simple Containerized Application Using Docker
Aim
To develop and run a simple web application inside a Docker container.

We'll create a basic HTML webpage and serve it using Nginx.

Step 1: Install Docker Desktop
If Docker is already installed and running, skip this step.

Download Docker Desktop for Windows from the official website:

Docker Desktop

Install it, open Docker Desktop, and wait until the Docker engine is running.

Check installation in Command Prompt:

docker --version
Then:

docker run hello-world
If you see Hello from Docker!, Docker is working.

Step 2: Create a project folder
Open Command Prompt and execute:

mkdir DockerLab
cd DockerLab
Step 3: Create the HTML file
Run:

notepad index.html
If Notepad asks whether to create the file, click Yes.

Paste this simple webpage:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Docker Lab</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Hello from Docker!</h1>
    <p>My first containerized application.</p>
</body>
</html>
Save the file and close Notepad.

Step 4: Create a Dockerfile
In Command Prompt, run:

notepad Dockerfile
Paste:

FROM nginx:alpine
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
Save the file as Dockerfile, without a .txt extension.

This tells Docker to:

Use the Nginx web server image.
Copy your HTML file into Nginx's website folder.
Step 5: Build the Docker image
In the same project folder, run:

docker build -t my-web-app .
Wait for the build to finish.

The command creates an image called my-web-app.

Step 6: Run the container
Execute:

docker run -d -p 8080:80 --name my-web-container my-web-app
Explanation:

-d runs the container in the background.
-p 8080:80 maps port 8080 on your computer to port 80 in the container.
--name gives the container a name.
Important: If Jenkins is already using port 8080, use -p 8081:80 instead.

Step 7: Open your application
Open Chrome and visit:

If you used port 8080:

Docker Web App

If you used port 8081:

Docker Web App

You should see:

Hello from Docker!

Step 8: Verify the container
Run:

docker ps
You should see my-web-container in the list.

To stop it:

docker stop my-web-container
To remove it:

docker rm my-web-container
Result
Successfully built a Docker image and deployed a simple HTML web application inside a container.

